Project: frameworks/base
Subject: [PATCH] wm: run task-snapshot GL on a single dedicated thread

Task snapshot conversion touches EGL (ImageReader / HardwareBuffer readback) and runs on
pooled binder threads, because WindowManager is binder-served
(SnapshotController -> TaskSnapshotController#createLowResSnapshot).

On a virtualized GPU the guest driver keeps per-thread EGL state that is released only at
thread exit. Under gfxstream that is a thread_local HostConnection owning a render-node fd
and host GL contexts. Binder threads never exit, so every pooled thread that ever converts
a snapshot strands one context for the life of system_server: one fresh binder thread
acquires a context per app launch, growing without bound until the host OOM-kills the VM.

copySWBitmap() is the single chokepoint for all snapshot GL, so funnelling it through one
long-lived thread bounds the cost to a single context for the process lifetime. The call
stays synchronous, so no caller changes; the work issues no binder calls, so blocking the
caller on it cannot deadlock.

Not architecture-specific: any gfxstream guest is affected. It goes unnoticed on hosts where
the per-context cost is negligible against available RAM.

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/TaskSnapshotConvertUtil.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/TaskSnapshotConvertUtil.java
index d9d5ba45fe46..10a12f15d2b7 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/TaskSnapshotConvertUtil.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/wm/TaskSnapshotConvertUtil.java
@@ -28,6 +28,10 @@ import android.media.ImageReader;
 import android.util.Slog;
 import android.window.TaskSnapshot;
 
+import java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException;
+import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
+import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
+
 /**
  * Utility class providing methods to convert snapshots between Bitmap formats and/or scale them.
  */
@@ -37,6 +41,27 @@ public class TaskSnapshotConvertUtil {
             ? "TaskSnapshotResolutionConvert" : TAG_WM;
 
     static TaskSnapshot convertSnapshotToLowRes(TaskSnapshot snapshot, float scaleFactor) {
+        // Hop to the dedicated GL thread. This must wrap the WHOLE conversion, not just
+        // copySWBitmap(): convertLowResSnapshot() below does a Bitmap.Config.HARDWARE copy,
+        // which is also a GPU upload and would otherwise stay on the caller's binder thread.
+        // Not nested with any other submit() to this single-thread executor -- copySWBitmap()
+        // runs inline from here, and its only other caller (SnapshotPersistQueue) is already
+        // on the long-lived persister thread.
+        try {
+            return glThread().submit(() -> convertSnapshotToLowResOnGlThread(
+                    snapshot, scaleFactor)).get();
+        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
+            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
+            Slog.e(TAG, "Interrupted converting task snapshot", e);
+            return null;
+        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
+            Slog.e(TAG, "Failed converting task snapshot", e.getCause());
+            return null;
+        }
+    }
+
+    private static TaskSnapshot convertSnapshotToLowResOnGlThread(TaskSnapshot snapshot,
+            float scaleFactor) {
         final Bitmap swBitmap = copySWBitmap(snapshot);
         if (swBitmap == null) {
             return null;
@@ -82,6 +107,39 @@ public class TaskSnapshotConvertUtil {
         return lowResSnapshot;
     }
 
+    /**
+     * Single thread on which all snapshot GL work runs.
+     *
+     * <p>Snapshot conversion touches EGL (via ImageReader / HardwareBuffer readback). On a
+     * virtualized GPU the guest driver creates per-thread EGL state -- under gfxstream a
+     * thread_local HostConnection owning a render-node fd and host GL contexts -- that is
+     * released only when the thread exits. Callers here are pooled binder threads
+     * (WindowManager is binder-served), which never exit, so doing the work inline leaks one
+     * such context per conversion, growing without bound until the host runs out of memory.
+     *
+     * <p>Funnelling every conversion through one long-lived thread bounds that to a single
+     * context for the lifetime of the process. The hop wraps the entire conversion -- both the
+     * ImageReader readback and the Bitmap.Config.HARDWARE upload -- because both touch the GPU.
+     * The work is CPU/GPU-only and issues no binder calls, so blocking the caller cannot
+     * deadlock, and it never re-enters this executor.
+     */
+    private static final Object sGlThreadLock = new Object();
+    private static ExecutorService sGlThread;
+
+    private static ExecutorService glThread() {
+        synchronized (sGlThreadLock) {
+            if (sGlThread == null) {
+                sGlThread = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor(
+                        r -> {
+                            final Thread t = new Thread(r, "TaskSnapshotGl");
+                            t.setDaemon(true);
+                            return t;
+                        });
+            }
+            return sGlThread;
+        }
+    }
+
     static Bitmap copySWBitmap(TaskSnapshot snapshot) {
         final int width = snapshot.getHardwareBufferWidth();
         final int height = snapshot.getHardwareBufferHeight();
